## Finding

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says one issue and one fix.
The export's executable classification identifies two distinct causes, so the shared subject
does not establish a duplicate. This review concerns the export dated 4 October 2026;
the reviewed date records this investigation, not a refresh of the underlying desk data.

<InvestigationSummary />

## Separate fixes

**Shared parser.** The parser always reads month first. A day-first tenant's ambiguous date
therefore changes meaning: Harbour Dental's R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July.
Use the tenant's date contract and test ambiguous dates in both directions.

**Form hint.** booking-v3 tells people to enter `MM/DD/YYYY`, while its form reads the
day-first tenant's convention. Copper Studio's R-41230 reports January 6 becoming June 1.
Make the displayed hint, validation and parser agree. A shared-parser fix alone does not fix
this misleading instruction.

The two issue drafts and complete report memberships are included in the repo under
`issues/` and `analysis/`. Counts below are computed from report IDs, tenant settings and
the supplied classifier at render time; they are not copied from intake tags.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Read the dates on the chart

Reports were received from April onward. The large July merge bar records when the desk
filed older reports under #4471, not when all those problems began. Compare received and
merged bars before assigning an incident onset or claiming a new regression.

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Five one-day observations remain an uncertainty

The desk's notes name R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344 and R-41620. Their bodies mention
one-day differences, June-only behavior, an invite arriving before booking, and different
days for Lisbon and Toronto. The exported classifier still places all five under parser.
Keep that fact visible in the count, and investigate timezone and event-timing evidence;
these reports do not alone establish a third confirmed root cause. Do not count them twice.

## Replace subject-only triage

The [subject-only rule](/kb/triage/dedupe/) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) were reviewed in June 2024.
Their accepted text is historical evidence, not proof that the rule matches the current
classifier. Equal subjects can cross causes; different subjects can share a cause.
The live examples below compute this distinction from tenant, channel and booking form.
Unknown tenant data is unresolved evidence, not permission to merge.

<DedupeExplorer />

<Callout>
Read the body, join the tenant's locale and booking form, then compare causes before merging.
Never substitute the reporter's browser locale or intake's guess for the tenant contract.
</Callout>

[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) proposes the replacement rule without
rewriting accepted decision 0001. The desk still owns adoption of that proposal.

## Engineering outcome: 5 October 2026

The course desk's engineering update closes #4471 on 5 October. It records two distinct
outcomes: **parser — FIXED**, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`; and
**hint — WON'T FIX**, because the booking-v3 form was retired. Retiring the form removes
that path; it does not establish that its misleading hint was repaired.

The parser ticket therefore closes as completed. The form-hint ticket closes as not planned,
with retirement as its reason. The historical counts remain the 4 October snapshot; a closed
ticket must not erase either the affected reports or the difference between these outcomes.

The five one-day observations remain visible as uncertainty. This update does not supply
individual explanations for those reports, so closure is not evidence of a proven timezone fix.

## Next review

The desk keeps working while this investigation runs. Before filing a final claim, check
the desk's latest evidence, regenerate the counts, and revise issues and this rendered page
if the report set changes. A valid snapshot is not a promise that today's queue is unchanged.

